1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,302
These swirling curls are traced by a single
function as it moves along one line.

2
00:00:05,352 --> 00:00:10,804
For more than 160 years, curves like them have
fascinated and frustrated mathematicians.

3
00:00:14,869 --> 00:00:17,150
Behind them hides the Riemann hypothesis:

4
00:00:17,200 --> 00:00:20,506
perhaps the most important unsolved
problem in mathematics,

5
00:00:20,556 --> 00:00:24,374
and one of the seven Millennium Problems,
worth one million dollars.

6
00:00:29,461 --> 00:00:33,341
If it is true, it reveals the deepest
pattern of the prime numbers.

7
00:00:33,391 --> 00:00:35,218
Let us build it up step by step:

8
00:00:35,268 --> 00:00:39,265
from counting, through infinite sums, all
the way to complex numbers.

9
00:00:44,054 --> 00:00:46,567
Every whole number is built from primes.

10
00:00:46,617 --> 00:00:50,541
Picture each number as a tower stacked
from its prime factors:

11
00:00:50,591 --> 00:00:52,848
twelve is two times two times three.

12
00:00:56,424 --> 00:00:58,978
The primes are the towers made
of a single block.

13
00:00:59,028 --> 00:01:02,803
More than two thousand years ago, Euclid
proved that they never run out.

14
00:01:07,461 --> 00:01:08,814
But where do they appear?

15
00:01:08,864 --> 00:01:10,498
They seem to pop up at random.

16
00:01:10,548 --> 00:01:15,045
Walking along the numbers, how many
single-block towers should you expect to see?

17
00:01:19,683 --> 00:01:23,916
As a teenager, Carl Friedrich Gauss
counted primes obsessively,

18
00:01:23,966 --> 00:01:26,023
eventually up to three million.

19
00:01:26,073 --> 00:01:28,810
Let π(x) be the number of primes up to x.

20
00:01:28,860 --> 00:01:31,121
It climbs one step at every prime.

21
00:01:34,424 --> 00:01:35,349
Now zoom out.

22
00:01:35,399 --> 00:01:38,873
Ten, a hundred, a thousand, a
hundred thousand:

23
00:01:38,923 --> 00:01:42,022
the jagged steps melt into a smooth curve.

24
00:01:44,128 --> 00:01:48,395
Gauss guessed that near x, roughly one
number in ln x is prime.

25
00:01:48,445 --> 00:01:52,506
Adding up that density gives the
logarithmic integral li(x).

26
00:01:52,556 --> 00:01:57,029
At this scale, the two curves are almost
impossible to tell apart.

27
00:02:00,276 --> 00:02:02,491
To go further we need infinite sums.

28
00:02:02,541 --> 00:02:05,512
One, plus one half, plus one
quarter, and so on:

29
00:02:05,562 --> 00:02:10,986
each new piece fills half of the remaining gap,
and the total converges to exactly two.

30
00:02:15,091 --> 00:02:18,831
The harmonic series also has shrinking terms:

31
00:02:18,881 --> 00:02:21,526
one, a half, a third, a quarter.

32
00:02:21,576 --> 00:02:24,137
Yet it creeps past every bound.

33
00:02:24,187 --> 00:02:27,675
To pass ten you already need 12,367 terms.

34
00:02:27,725 --> 00:02:28,686
It diverges.

35
00:02:30,136 --> 00:02:37,002
In 1735, Leonhard Euler cracked the sum of one over
the squares and found π squared over

36
00:02:37,052 --> 00:02:37,316
six.

37
00:02:37,366 --> 00:02:41,403
Squares on one side, the circle
number on the other.

38
00:02:42,951 --> 00:02:44,608
Now let the exponent vary.

39
00:02:44,658 --> 00:02:47,300
Call it s, and you get the zeta function:

40
00:02:47,350 --> 00:02:50,189
zeta of s is the sum of one over n to the s.

41
00:02:50,239 --> 00:02:53,275
For every s greater than one
the sum converges.

42
00:02:56,876 --> 00:03:00,360
Euler then found that the very same
function is a product,

43
00:03:00,410 --> 00:03:02,309
with one factor for every prime.

44
00:03:02,359 --> 00:03:06,574
Multiply the factors out, and every whole
number appears exactly once,

45
00:03:06,624 --> 00:03:08,584
assembled from its prime factors.

46
00:03:13,395 --> 00:03:14,922
This product is the bridge.

47
00:03:14,972 --> 00:03:18,718
Whatever the zeta function does, the
primes are hidden inside it.

48
00:03:22,284 --> 00:03:27,767
In 1859, Gauss’s former student Bernhard
Riemann had a radical idea:

49
00:03:27,817 --> 00:03:30,941
feed the zeta function complex numbers.

50
00:03:32,391 --> 00:03:35,898
A complex number has a real part
and an imaginary part,

51
00:03:35,948 --> 00:03:38,809
so it lives in a plane rather than on a line.

52
00:03:38,859 --> 00:03:42,042
Multiplying by i turns every
point a quarter turn:

53
00:03:42,092 --> 00:03:43,530
i squared is minus one.

54
00:03:47,428 --> 00:03:52,749
With a complex exponent, every term of the sum
becomes a small rotating arrow.

55
00:03:52,799 --> 00:03:58,533
Placed tip to tail, the arrows for s equal to two
plus three i spiral in towards one

56
00:03:58,583 --> 00:03:58,946
point:

57
00:03:58,996 --> 00:04:01,563
the value of zeta at two plus three i.

58
00:04:04,909 --> 00:04:09,156
But push the real part below one and the
spiral never settles down.

59
00:04:09,206 --> 00:04:10,760
It curls outward forever.

60
00:04:10,810 --> 00:04:13,004
Here the sum simply makes no sense.

61
00:04:16,465 --> 00:04:18,404
Riemann found a way around this.

62
00:04:18,454 --> 00:04:23,562
Analytic continuation extends a function beyond
the region where its formula works,

63
00:04:23,612 --> 00:04:26,048
and it can do so in only one smooth way.

64
00:04:30,095 --> 00:04:33,605
Here every point of the plane is
coloured by the value of zeta:

65
00:04:33,655 --> 00:04:36,317
the hue shows its direction,
the rings its size.

66
00:04:36,367 --> 00:04:39,708
Where the sum converges, on the
right, the picture is fixed.

67
00:04:39,758 --> 00:04:42,590
Riemann’s extension fills in the
rest of the plane.

68
00:04:48,761 --> 00:04:50,778
Only one point stays forbidden.

69
00:04:50,828 --> 00:04:54,444
At s equal to one, the function
shoots off to infinity.

70
00:04:57,206 --> 00:05:00,313
Dive in, and the values grow
beyond every bound:

71
00:05:00,363 --> 00:05:04,259
near s equal to one, zeta behaves
like one over s minus one.

72
00:05:04,309 --> 00:05:08,403
The pole is not a place you can reach,
only a road to infinity.

73
00:05:12,021 --> 00:05:15,458
In this new territory, zeta can
take the value zero.

74
00:05:15,508 --> 00:05:20,018
At minus two, minus four, minus six and
so on lie the trivial zeros.

75
00:05:20,068 --> 00:05:21,695
They are easy to explain.

76
00:05:24,909 --> 00:05:27,078
The other zeros are far more mysterious.

77
00:05:27,128 --> 00:05:31,683
All of them lie in the critical strip, where the
real part is between zero and one,

78
00:05:31,733 --> 00:05:34,179
and Riemann showed there are infinitely many.

79
00:05:39,502 --> 00:05:41,504
Lift the strip into a landscape.

80
00:05:41,554 --> 00:05:46,121
Each ridge traces the size of zeta along
one vertical line in the strip.

81
00:05:46,171 --> 00:05:47,211
Watch the ground:

82
00:05:47,261 --> 00:05:49,199
only one ridge ever touches it.

83
00:05:52,983 --> 00:05:55,810
It is the line with real part
exactly one half.

84
00:05:55,860 --> 00:06:00,400
Riemann conjectured that every non-trivial
zero lies on this critical line.

85
00:06:00,450 --> 00:06:02,297
That is the Riemann hypothesis.

86
00:06:06,465 --> 00:06:09,185
Why should the zeros matter for the primes?

87
00:06:09,235 --> 00:06:12,278
Take the prime staircase and
change it slightly:

88
00:06:12,328 --> 00:06:16,466
step up by log p at every prime p, and
at every power of a prime.

89
00:06:20,169 --> 00:06:23,055
Riemann’s explicit formula
rebuilds this staircase.

90
00:06:23,105 --> 00:06:26,107
It starts with a smooth straight
line, the main term.

91
00:06:29,946 --> 00:06:35,442
Then every zero adds a wave, a harmonic whose
frequency is the zero’s height on the

92
00:06:35,492 --> 00:06:36,377
critical line.

93
00:06:36,427 --> 00:06:38,515
One zero, two, five, ten, forty:

94
00:06:38,565 --> 00:06:41,120
the waves begin to carve out the steps.

95
00:06:44,613 --> 00:06:47,601
With all infinitely many zeros
the match is exact.

96
00:06:47,651 --> 00:06:50,577
The primes are the sum of the
music of the zeros,

97
00:06:50,627 --> 00:06:54,951
and the hypothesis says this music is as
balanced as it can possibly be.

98
00:06:59,428 --> 00:07:02,814
Computers have now checked more
than ten trillion zeros.

99
00:07:02,864 --> 00:07:06,434
Every single one of them sits
exactly on the critical line.

100
00:07:10,021 --> 00:07:12,370
But no finite search can reach infinity.

101
00:07:12,420 --> 00:07:15,728
A single zero off the line would
destroy the hypothesis,

102
00:07:15,778 --> 00:07:17,828
and only a proof can rule that out.

103
00:07:21,872 --> 00:07:25,884
So the question Riemann raised in
1859 is still open.

104
00:07:25,934 --> 00:07:28,413
Do all the zeros lie on one line?

105
00:07:28,463 --> 00:07:32,474
Whoever proves it will finally
understand the primes.

